 ],
 "caption": "A woman walked into the National Gallery with a meat cleaver up her sleeve.\n\nOn 10 March 1914, suffragette Mary Richardson attacked Velázquez's Rokeby Venus, his only surviving female nude, which Britain had raised a fortune to buy in 1906. The guard mistook the breaking glass for a skylight, then slipped on the polished floor.\n\nHer statement: she had tried to destroy \"the most beautiful woman in mythological history\" in protest at the government's treatment of Emmeline Pankhurst, arrested the day before. Decades later she gave another reason: she didn't like the way men visitors gaped at it all day.\n\nThe painting was restored and still hangs in London.\n\n🖤 Was it vandalism or protest?",
 "caption_short": "1914: a suffragette took a meat cleaver to Velázquez's Venus. Decades later she admitted a reason she hadn't given at the time. 🖤",
 "youtube_title": "Why did a suffragette take a meat cleaver to this painting?",
